<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1907 · Egypt &amp; North Africa</p><h1 class="sg-title">Biladi, Biladi, Biladi</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ar" dir="rtl">بلادي بلادي بلادي</bdi> / <bdi lang="ar-Latn">Bilādī, Bilādī, Bilādī</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The national anthem of Egypt, a song of love for the homeland whose title means &quot;My Homeland, My Homeland, My Homeland&quot;.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Muhammad Yunis al-Qady</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Sayed Darwish</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Egyptians; the Egyptian national movement</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Egypt</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>chorus drawn from a speech of Mustafa Kamel in 1907; adopted as the national anthem in 1979</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Muhammad Yunis al-Qady and music by Sayed Darwish, early 20th century)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Bilādī, Bilādī, Bilādī&quot; (&quot;My Homeland, My Homeland, My Homeland&quot;) is the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Its words were written by Muhammad Yunis al-Qady and its music composed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayed-darwish">Sayed Darwish</a>, a composer who kept close ties with early leaders of Egypt's national movement for independence, among them Mustafa Kamel. The chorus is drawn from one of Kamel's most famous nationalist speeches, given in 1907, in which he declared his love for his country.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> had several anthems before it. The first, known as the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khedive-of-egypt">Khedive</a>'s Special Salute&quot; or &quot;Salam Affandina&quot;, dated from the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isma-il-pasha">Khedive Ismail</a>: it was played at the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> and adopted in 1871, an instrumental piece without official words whose most likely author was Giuseppe Pugioli, an Italian cornetist who had come to Egypt to perform <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Verdi</a>'s opera Aida and stayed. Without words it never became popular inside Egypt; after the revolution of 1919 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-shawqi">Ahmad Shawqi</a> won a competition held in 1920 to give it lyrics, but his text was not taken up. &quot;Eslami ya Misr&quot;, written by Mustafa Sadiq Al-Rafei and composed by Safar Ali, was the anthem from 1923 to 1936. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farouk-of-egypt">King Farouk</a> restored the royal anthem in 1936, and after the end of the monarchy it was kept under a new name, the Egyptian Republican anthem.</p><p>In 1960 Republican Decree No. 143 adopted Kamal El-Tawil's melody for &quot;Wallah Zaman Ya Selahy&quot;, a song with words by Salah Jahin for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umm-kulthum">Umm Kulthum</a> that had become popular in 1956 during the Tripartite Aggression; it was played without words. It remained the anthem until 1979, and served too as the anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-arab-republic">United Arab Republic</a> and, from 1963 to 1981, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>.</p><p>In 1979 Republican Decree No. 149 made &quot;Bilādī, Bilādī, Bilādī&quot; the national anthem, in an arrangement by the musician Mohamed Abdel Wahab made at the direction of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anwar-sadat">Anwar Sadat</a>. A decree of December 1982, No. 590, laid down that the words of its first verse accompany the music at popular and national celebrations, while the music alone is played to welcome presidents and foreign delegations. An unofficial Coptic version, not codified in law, circulates widely online.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>An anthem of love and devotion to the homeland, built on the patriotic language of Mustafa Kamel's nationalist speech. The mood is warm and solemn.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Arabic</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Arabic</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">بِلَادِي بِلَادِي بِلَادِي</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Bilādī bilādī bilādī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My country, my country, my country,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">لَكِ حُبِّي وَفُؤَادِي</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Laki hubbī wa-fuʾādī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>yours are my love and my heart.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">بِلَادِي بِلَادِي بِلَادِي</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Bilādī bilādī bilādī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My country, my country, my country,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">لَكِ حُبِّي وَفُؤَادِي</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Laki hubbī wa-fuʾādī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>yours are my love and my heart.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">مَصْرِ يَا أُمِّ الْبِلَادْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Masri yā ummi al-bilād</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Egypt, O mother of the lands,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">أَنْتِ غَايَتِي وَالْمُرَادْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Anti ghāyatī wa-al-murād</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you are my aim and my desire,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">وَعَلَى كُلِّ الْعِبَادْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Wa-ʿalá kulli al-ʿibād</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and upon all people</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">كَمْ لِنِيلِكْ مِنْ أَيَادِي</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Kam li-nīlik min ayādī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>how many favours your Nile has bestowed!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">مَصْرِ أَنْتِ أَغْلَى دُرَّة</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Masri anti aghlá durrah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Egypt, you are the most precious pearl,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">فُوقْ جَبِينِ الدَّهْرِ غُرَّة</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Fūq jabīni al-dahri ghurrah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a white blaze on the forehead of time.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">يَا بِلَادِي عِيشِي حُرَّة</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Yā bilādī ʿīshī hurrah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O my country, live free</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">وَاسْلَمِي رَغْمَ الْأَعَادِي</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Wa-islamī raghma al-aʿādī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and stay safe in spite of the enemies.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">مَصْرِ أَوْلَادِكْ كِرَامْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Masri awlādik kirām</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Egypt, your children are noble,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">أَوْفِيَاء يَرْعُوا الزِّمَامْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Awfiyāʾ yarʿū al-zimām</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>faithful, they keep hold of the reins.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">نَحْنُ حَرْبٌ وَسَلَامْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Nahnu harbun wa-salām</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We are war and peace,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">وَفِدَاكِي يَا بِلَادِي</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Wa-fidākī yā bilādī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and we are your ransom, O my country.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Muhammad Yunis al-Qady (early 20th century); the Arabic text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biladi%2C_Biladi%2C_Biladi" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (ALA-LC).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (5)</summary><ul><li>Romanization: ALA-LC without the dots under letters, with ʿ and ʾ, rendering the words as the vocalised text writes them, including its Egyptian forms: 'Masri' (مَصْرِ, with a, as Egyptians say it), 'fūq' for fawqa, the pausal 'nīlik', 'awlādik' and 'fidākī'.</li><li>line 2.4: 'ayādī', literally 'hands', is the classical word for favours or benefactions.</li><li>line 3.2: 'ghurrah' is the white blaze on a horse's forehead, hence the finest and most conspicuous thing.</li><li>line 4.2: 'yarʿū al-zimām', literally 'they tend the rein (or halter)'; the idiom means they keep faith and hold to their duty.</li><li>line 4.4: 'fidākī', 'your ransom': we give ourselves for you.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Arrangement by Mohamed Abdel Wahab</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1979</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new arrangement for the national anthem</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Coptic version</span><span class="sg-chain-h">unofficial version, widely circulated online</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Speech of Mustafa Kamel</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1907</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the chorus is drawn from it</span></li></ul></section>
